menu "shiroko Tab5 example"
    choice SHIROKO_TAB5_BOARD
        prompt "Board"
        default SHIROKO_TAB5_BOARD_TAB5
        help
            Tab5 shows the frame buffer on its MIPI-DSI panel. Headless (an ESP32-P4 board without a panel, such as the
            M5Stack Unit PoE-P4) draws into a PSRAM frame buffer of the same size and format that nothing shows.

        config SHIROKO_TAB5_BOARD_TAB5
            bool "M5Stack Tab5"
        config SHIROKO_TAB5_BOARD_HEADLESS
            bool "Headless ESP32-P4"
    endchoice

    choice SHIROKO_TAB5_SCANOUT
        prompt "Scan-out on the headless board"
        depends on SHIROKO_TAB5_BOARD_HEADLESS
        default SHIROKO_TAB5_SCANOUT_NONE
        help
            Streams the frame buffer out of the MIPI-DSI host with a Tab5 panel's timing, nothing attached, so the
            PSRAM reads of the panel's scan-out load the board as on that Tab5.

        config SHIROKO_TAB5_SCANOUT_NONE
            bool "None"
        config SHIROKO_TAB5_SCANOUT_ILI9881C
            bool "ILI9881C board (48 Hz)"
        config SHIROKO_TAB5_SCANOUT_ST7121
            bool "ST7121 board (57 Hz)"
    endchoice

    config SHIROKO_TAB5_BANDS
        int "Internal-RAM bands"
        range 1 2
        default 2
        help
            The screen is drawn 16 rows at a time into bands of 40 KiB of internal RAM each, which the PPA turns
            into the frame buffer. 2 (default) draws one band while the PPA turns the other. 1 leaves the app
            40 KiB more internal RAM, but the CPU waits on the PPA: on the ST7121 Tab5 (uncapped, 216 KiB budget)
            the scenes took about 14 % longer (p50 summed), and a scrolling screen fell into periodic 60 ms frames.

    config SHIROKO_TAB5_ALL_MODES
        bool "Run every drawing mode"
        default n
        help
            Runs each scene composed in PSRAM (A), with one band (B) and, with two bands, with two (C); only the
            mode of SHIROKO_TAB5_BANDS when off.

    config SHIROKO_TAB5_FPS_CAP
        int "Frame-rate cap (frames per second, 0 = uncapped)"
        range 0 1000
        default 30
        help
            Sets shr_context_desc.min_frame_interval_ns to 1 s / this value. 0 measures the renderer uncapped.

    config SHIROKO_TAB5_KEEP_SCREENS
        int "Keep slots, in screens of rows"
        range 1 8
        default 4
        help
            The software driver keeps rendered rows for this many screens (45 slots of 40 KiB each, about 1.8 MiB
            of PSRAM per screen), so rows switching among that many screens or looks are copied back instead of
            drawn. 4 (default) fits rows cycling through four looks (restyle4: stores 180 over 60 frames, frame
            p50 28.8 ms); 3 saves 1.8 MiB and runs the other scenes as fast (restyle4 p50 47.6 ms); 2 saves 3.6 MiB
            (restyle4 p50 55.7 ms).

    config SHIROKO_TAB5_INTERNAL_BUDGET_KB
        int "Internal RAM for the renderer's per-frame memory (KiB)"
        range 0 1024
        default 64
        help
            Memory the renderer uses every frame (SHR_ALLOC_HOT: rows of commands, the band command list, per-frame
            state) goes to internal RAM while it fits in this many KiB, the rest of it and all other renderer memory
            to PSRAM; the rest of internal RAM stays with the application. 64 (default) leaves the app about
            360 KiB free at the end of each scene on Tab5, 165 KiB more than 216, for frames about 5 % longer
            uncapped (p50 summed over the scenes; Hangul scenes 4-5 ms). 0 puts it all in PSRAM.
endmenu
